    expectedAnswer: "Context is excellent for low-frequency cross-cutting values. Frequent, broad updates can cause many consumers to render, so split providers or use an external store when measured fan-out becomes costly.",
    deepExplanation: "Example:\n\n```ts\nimport { createContext, useContext, useMemo } from \"react\";\n\ntype Session = { userId: string } | null;\nconst SessionContext = createContext<Session>(null);\n\nexport function SessionProvider({\n  session,\n  children,\n}: { session: Session; children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  const value = useMemo(() => session, [session]);\n  return (\n    <SessionContext.Provider value={value}>\n      {children}\n    </SessionContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nexport const useSession = () => useContext(SessionContext);\n```\n\nProduction pattern:\n\n```ts\n// Prefer immutable updates and keep derived values out of stored state.\nconst state = { user: { id: \"u1\", name: \"Asha\" } };\nconst next = {\n  ...state,\n  user: { ...state.user, name: \"Maya\" },\n};\n\nconsole.log(state.user.name); // Asha\nconsole.log(next.user.name);  // Maya\n```\n\nWhy it matters: Prevents duplicated or conflicting state. Makes rendering behavior predictable. Enables testable business logic and observable production behavior. Supports scaling from a single feature to large multi-team applications.",
    productionExample: "```ts\n// Prefer immutable updates and keep derived values out of stored state.\nconst state = { user: { id: \"u1\", name: \"Asha\" } };\nconst next = {\n  ...state,\n  user: { ...state.user, name: \"Maya\" },\n};\n\nconsole.log(state.user.name); // Asha\nconsole.log(next.user.name);  // Maya\n```",
    bestPractices: ["Prefer explicit, readable code."],
